1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
This is Doing and Feeling.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,240
Episode eight: The Case for Illusion.

3
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:09,130
Right now, without moving your eyes, ask
yourself how much of what you see is in

4
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:12,980
color. For almost everyone, the
answer comes back instantly:

5
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:19,170
all of it. In 2020, the psychologists
Michael Cohen, Thomas Botch, and Caroline

6
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,040
Robertson tested that confidence.

7
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:26,210
Participants wore virtual reality headsets
with eye tracking and explored immersive

8
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:30,860
scenes: a historic site, a street
dance, an orchestra rehearsal.

9
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:35,130
The software kept color only where each
person was looking and drained it from

10
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:38,080
everything else, turning the
periphery black and white.

11
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:40,360
Most participants never noticed.

12
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:46,170
In the most extreme condition, with color
left in only a small central patch, less

13
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:50,680
than five percent of the visual field,
nearly a third of them still did not notice.

14
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:53,100
That's a fact about peripheral vision.

15
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:55,420
Nobody thinks the eye is perfect.

16
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:57,960
The eye isn't what failed, though.

17
00:00:58,300 --> 00:00:59,840
What failed was the report.

18
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:04,890
People were asked whether anything had
changed in what they saw, and most reported

19
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:08,650
nothing amiss, although much of their
visual field had lost its color.

20
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,780
There are two ways to read that.

21
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:15,490
The realist reading says the brain filled
in the color, so the experience really was

22
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,800
colored, and introspection got the experience
right while the world was different.

23
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:24,230
The other reading says people's judgments
about their experience ran ahead of

24
00:01:24,280 --> 00:01:30,130
anything going on. Either way, the sense
of a rich, uniform, colorful visual field

25
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:34,480
turns out to be a construction, and
introspection did not flag it as one.

26
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,180
Today's question is how far that goes.

27
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:42,290
Could introspection be wrong not just about
the details of experience, but about its

28
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:46,640
most basic character, the very thing
that makes the hard problem seem hard?

29
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,730
That is the claim of illusionism, and today
it gets the strongest hearing I can give

30
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:54,180
it, followed by its hardest objections.

31
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:59,700
Start with what the view actually says,
because it's routinely caricatured.

32
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:05,830
The modern statement is Keith Frankish's
2016 paper, Illusionism as a Theory of

33
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:10,370
Consciousness, published as a target article
in the Journal of Consciousness Studies

34
00:02:10,420 --> 00:02:14,270
with a symposium of replies,
later collected in a book.

35
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:19,130
Frankish draws a line between two
things that ordinary talk runs together.

36
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:23,250
On one side is consciousness in
the everyday, functional sense.

37
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:28,160
You're awake. You see the tomato, you can
attend to it, think about it, report it.

38
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:33,600
When you stub your toe, a state arises that
registers damage, seizes your attention,

39
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:35,280
and makes you want it to stop.

40
00:02:35,980 --> 00:02:37,640
Illusionists deny none of this.

41
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:41,760
On the other side is phenomenal
consciousness in the philosopher's sense:

42
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:47,620
special qualities of experience, qualia,
that are supposed to be intrinsic, private,

43
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:49,560
ineffable, and known directly.

44
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:54,310
The redness of the red, considered as
something over and above everything the

45
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:57,800
experience does. That is
what illusionism denies.

46
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,480
Frankish's claim is that there
are no such phenomenal properties.

47
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:05,140
What exist are what he calls
quasi-phenomenal properties:

48
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:10,110
physical properties of brain states that
introspection represents as having that

49
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:11,680
special phenomenal character.

50
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:14,100
The representation is real.

51
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:16,400
The character it attributes is not.

52
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:19,620
So what am I supposed to
say about a toothache?

53
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:21,420
That it only seems to hurt?

54
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:24,900
It hurts. Everything that
hurting does is real:

55
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:29,240
the alarm, the distraction, the
misery, the plans to see a dentist.

56
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:34,200
What the illusionist denies is a further
private glow that floats free of all that,

57
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:38,230
which introspection seems to point at when
you attend to the pain in a philosophical

58
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:40,440
mood. The hurting is real.

59
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:43,560
The extra, ineffable
quality is the illusion.

60
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:47,580
Frankish also distinguishes
strong illusionism from weak.

61
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:52,800
Weak illusionism says phenomenal properties
exist but aren't quite as they seem.

62
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:57,570
He argues that position is unstable,
because once you strip away the strange

63
00:03:57,620 --> 00:04:01,600
features, what's left is functional
and representational anyway.

64
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:03,790
So he defends the strong version.

65
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:09,140
And he insists on a positive research
program, which he calls the illusion problem:

66
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:14,650
explain why introspection represents our
states as having phenomenal properties, and

67
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:17,020
why that representation is so compelling.

68
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,400
Compelling is the key word.

69
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:24,710
In the Müller-Lyer illusion, two equal lines
with arrowheads pointing in or out look

70
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:26,840
unequal even after you've measured them.

71
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:30,220
Knowing the explanation
doesn't switch an illusion off.

72
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:35,310
So if illusionism is true, your sense of
an inner glow should survive your accepting

73
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:40,410
the theory, and its stubborn obviousness
is not, by itself, evidence against the

74
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:47,010
view. The view's deepest roots are in
Daniel Dennett, who died in 2024 and who

75
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:48,920
spent half a century on this project.

76
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:55,190
In a 1988 paper, Quining Qualia, Dennett
went after the very concept of qualia as

77
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:58,900
philosophers define it. His
method was the intuition pump:

78
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:02,840
a carefully designed story that
shows you how your intuitions work.

79
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:07,640
The best known involves two professional
coffee tasters at the same company.

80
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:12,220
Both have tasted the house blend for
years, and both have stopped liking it.

81
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:16,500
One says the coffee tastes the same to
him, but his standards have changed.

82
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:21,330
The other says his taste buds must have
changed, so the coffee now tastes different

83
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:23,580
to him. Dennett pressed the question:

84
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:26,160
what could possibly settle
which of them is right?

85
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:28,890
Perhaps there is no fact
of the matter at all.

86
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:34,150
And if a concept generates questions with
no possible answers, maybe the concept is

87
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:39,830
confused. His 1991 book, Consciousness
Explained, attacked a picture he called the

88
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:43,950
Cartesian theater: the idea that somewhere
in the brain there's a place where it all

89
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,370
comes together, a screen watched by an
inner audience, and that a content becomes

90
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:50,380
conscious at the moment it arrives there.

91
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:54,440
The brain, he argued, has no
such place and no such moment.

92
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,540
What it has are multiple drafts:

93
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:00,180
parallel streams of content,
constantly edited and revised.

94
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:03,010
His favorite example was color phi.

95
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:09,650
In 1976, Paul Kolers and Michael von Grünau
flashed a red dot and then, quickly, a

96
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:11,560
green dot a short distance away.

97
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,870
People see one dot moving and turning green
partway along its path, before the green

98
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,860
dot has even appeared. So
how did the brain know?

99
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:24,410
Either it held the experience back and
edited it before release, which Dennett

100
00:06:24,460 --> 00:06:28,920
called the Stalinesque story, or you saw
it correctly and your memory was rewritten,

101
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:34,050
the Orwellian story. Dennett's provocative
conclusion was that there is no fact

102
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:38,310
about which is true, because there is no
finish line in the brain where experience

103
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:41,760
officially happens. No
finish line, no theater.

104
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:47,180
A distributed system has no global clock
either, and things still happen in it.

105
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:50,120
They do, and Dennett would
take the analogy gladly.

106
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:55,050
Ask when a write really happened, apart
from what the logs say and what the other

107
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:57,580
nodes later see, and
there may be no answer.

108
00:06:57,880 --> 00:07:00,360
His claim is that the brain is like that.

109
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:02,360
Things happen in it all the time.

110
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:07,040
What's missing is a single moment when
one of them officially becomes experience.

111
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:10,660
In place of the theater, Dennett
offered a method and a slogan.

112
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:13,220
The method was heterophenomenology:

113
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:18,210
take people's reports about their experience
with total seriousness, as data about

114
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:22,430
how things seem to them, without assuming
the reports are accurate descriptions of

115
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:25,980
what's happening inside. The
slogan was fame in the brain.

116
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:31,210
A content becomes conscious the way a
person becomes famous, by winning influence

117
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:34,580
over the rest of the system,
not by entering a special room.

118
00:07:34,780 --> 00:07:36,840
And he borrowed an image from computing:

119
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:41,280
the user illusion. Your laptop
shows you folders and a trash can.

120
00:07:41,460 --> 00:07:46,750
There are no tiny folders on the chip,
but the interface is real, useful, and the

121
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:48,810
only way you ever deal
with what's underneath.

122
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:53,400
On this view, the felt inner world
is the brain's interface to itself.

123
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:59,240
Illusionism needs evidence that introspection
can be wrong about our own minds.

124
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:05,310
There's a lot of it. In 1977, Richard Nisbett
and Timothy Wilson published a classic

125
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:08,020
paper with the title Telling
More Than We Can Know.

126
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:13,710
In one study, shoppers choosing among
identical items laid out in a row tended to

127
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:14,900
pick the one on the right.

128
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:19,720
Asked why, they cited quality, and
denied that position had mattered.

129
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:24,640
People confidently report the causes of
their own choices, and are often wrong.

130
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:30,050
The philosopher Eric Schwitzgebel has
argued that the problem goes deeper, to

131
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:36,650
experience itself. In a 2008 paper on the
unreliability of naive introspection, he

132
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:41,300
collected basic questions about current
experience on which careful people disagree:

133
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:46,760
how clear the periphery of the visual field
is, whether emotions have a bodily feel,

134
00:08:47,020 --> 00:08:48,820
whether thought has a sensory character.

135
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:53,750
These are questions about what experience
is like right now, and introspection gives

136
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:59,010
conflicting answers. The psychologist Russell
Hurlburt has spent decades on a method

137
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:01,340
called descriptive experience sampling.

138
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:06,390
A beeper goes off at random moments, and
you describe exactly what was in your

139
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:08,030
experience just before it.

140
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:12,690
In one study of thirty people, inner speech
showed up in about a quarter of samples

141
00:09:12,740 --> 00:09:15,220
on average, but with enormous variation:

142
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:19,580
some people reported none at all,
others three quarters of the time.

143
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:24,950
Hurlburt's long-running conclusion is that
people's armchair generalizations about

144
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,650
their own inner lives are far less
trustworthy than careful reports of specific

145
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:35,370
moments. And in 2015, the neurologist
Adam Zeman gave a name to aphantasia:

146
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:38,500
the lifelong absence of
voluntary visual imagery.

147
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:43,180
Some people discover only as adults that
when others say they can picture a face,

148
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:48,390
they mean it literally. A difference as
large as having inner pictures or not can go

149
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:51,900
unnoticed for decades, because
everyone uses the same words.

150
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:55,700
In episode ten, we'll add
choice blindness to this file.

151
00:09:55,980 --> 00:10:00,110
All of that shows introspection
is unreliable about details.

152
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:04,140
It doesn't show it's wrong about
there being experience at all.

153
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:08,480
You're sliding from "sometimes mistaken"
to "mistaken about everything."

154
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:13,140
That's exactly the right objection,
and the illusionist has to answer it.

155
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:16,800
Local unreliability doesn't
establish global error.

156
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:19,560
What illusionists add is a second step.

157
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:24,050
They argue that we can explain why we'd
believe in phenomenal properties even if

158
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:28,970
there were none, and that if such an
explanation works, believing in them anyway is

159
00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:31,820
like believing in a ghost after
you've found the projector.

160
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:33,980
Which brings us to the meta-problem.

161
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:38,300
Last episode ended with
Chalmers's meta-problem:

162
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:40,460
why do we think there is a hard problem?

163
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:46,070
His 2018 paper, which drew a large set
of replies, calls the culprits problem

164
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:48,720
intuitions. He sorts them into families.

165
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:53,230
Explanatory intuitions: experience
can't be explained physically.

166
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:57,020
Metaphysical intuitions:
experience isn't physical.

167
00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:59,540
Knowledge intuitions, like Mary's.

168
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:01,640
Modal intuitions, like zombies.

169
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:03,660
And these intuitions run deep.

170
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:08,590
The developmental psychologist Paul Bloom
has argued that children are natural

171
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:12,240
dualists, readily treating
minds as separable from bodies.

172
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:14,100
What might the answer look like?

173
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:17,980
The answers I find most promising
involve introspective models.

174
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:23,150
The brain represents its own perceptual
states in a simplified way, as primitive

175
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:27,370
qualities with no visible structure,
because it has no need to represent the

176
00:11:27,420 --> 00:11:31,950
underlying machinery. A system with a
model like that would find its own states

177
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,270
ineffable, intrinsic, and impossible to
derive from anything physical, because the

178
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:39,400
model offers nothing to derive them from.

179
00:11:39,780 --> 00:11:43,780
Then comes the twist that makes the
meta-problem dangerous for realists.

180
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:48,690
Suppose we find a complete physical
explanation of why we say there's a hard

181
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:53,160
problem, and that explanation never
needs to mention experience itself.

182
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:58,010
Then our beliefs about experience would be
explained without experience playing any

183
00:11:58,060 --> 00:12:02,590
role. That threatens to debunk them, the
way learning that a belief was produced by

184
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:05,900
a process indifferent to its
truth undermines the belief.

185
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:08,060
Chalmers takes this seriously.

186
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:13,330
Either the illusionist is right, or the
realist must show that experience itself

187
00:12:13,380 --> 00:12:15,700
plays some role in
producing our talk about it.

188
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:21,210
That's why he treats illusionism as a serious
rival to his own view, not as a fringe

189
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:23,660
position. Here's what bugs me.

190
00:12:23,860 --> 00:12:28,560
Any language model trained on human
text will reproduce problem intuitions.

191
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:34,210
Ask one about its experience and it may
tell you its inner life is mysterious and

192
00:12:34,260 --> 00:12:39,120
hard to put into words. Does that make
the meta-problem easier, or harder?

193
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:44,410
Both, I think. Easier, because it proves
that problem-intuition behavior can be

194
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:46,600
produced by a mechanism we can inspect.

195
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:51,450
Harder, because it shows the behavior can
arise for reasons that have nothing to do

196
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:56,010
with having experience, or even with
having an introspective model, simply by

197
00:12:56,060 --> 00:12:57,880
learning from people who talk this way.

198
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:01,740
In a language model, the reports
alone tell you very little.

199
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:07,410
That turns out to be one of the central
obstacles in episodes twelve and thirteen.

200
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:11,400
Several researchers have tried to
say what the mechanism actually is.

201
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:15,440
The most concrete is Michael
Graziano's attention schema theory.

202
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:20,850
The brain constantly controls its own
attention, and good control requires a model

203
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:25,190
of the thing being controlled, the
good regulator idea from episode four.

204
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:30,250
So the brain builds a simplified model of
its attention, and that model depicts not

205
00:13:30,300 --> 00:13:33,410
neurons competing for signal
strength but something simpler:

206
00:13:33,460 --> 00:13:36,660
an inner essence that
grasps things, an awareness.

207
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:41,830
When you say you're aware of the cup,
Graziano argues, you're reporting the contents

208
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:46,990
of that schema. It's the closest thing
illusionism has to a wiring diagram, though

209
00:13:47,040 --> 00:13:49,800
Graziano himself dislikes
the word illusion.

210
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:55,800
Attention is real, he says, and awareness
is the brain's useful caricature of it.

211
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:59,430
In episode eleven we'll treat
it as a scientific theory.

212
00:13:59,480 --> 00:14:05,130
In 2021, Andrew Wilterson and Graziano
built a deep reinforcement learning agent

213
00:14:05,180 --> 00:14:09,140
that had to control a simulated spotlight
of attention to catch a falling ball.

214
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:13,960
Given an attention schema, a model of
its own attention, it learned the task.

215
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:17,300
With the schema disabled,
its performance collapsed.

216
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:20,260
That shows a functional
reason to have such a model.

217
00:14:20,540 --> 00:14:25,570
It says nothing about whether the agent
experiences anything, and Graziano doesn't

218
00:14:25,620 --> 00:14:29,950
claim it does. Thomas Metzinger's Being No
One argues that there is no such thing as

219
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:34,870
a self. What exists is a phenomenal self-model
that the brain cannot recognize as a

220
00:14:34,920 --> 00:14:39,150
model. It's transparent, like a window
you look through rather than at, so you

221
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:42,000
experience yourself as a self
rather than as a model of one.

222
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:47,830
Douglas Hofstadter's Gödel, Escher, Bach,
and later I Am a Strange Loop, argue that

223
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:52,390
a self is a strange loop: a pattern in
a symbol-processing system that comes to

224
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:57,170
represent itself. The psychologist Nicholas
Humphrey is a fellow traveler rather

225
00:14:57,220 --> 00:14:59,080
than a card-carrying illusionist.

226
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:05,730
In Soul Dust and in Sentience, from 2022,
he describes phenomenal consciousness as a

227
00:15:05,780 --> 00:15:10,810
kind of magical show the brain stages for
itself, one that may have evolved because

228
00:15:10,860 --> 00:15:13,000
it makes being alive
feel worth the trouble.

229
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:17,650
But in his reply to Frankish, he resisted
the word illusion and called his view

230
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:23,120
phenomenal surrealism. The show is
constructed, he thinks, but it isn't a mistake.

231
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:26,480
It's the brain's own artwork,
and it matters to us.

232
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:30,520
And Geoffrey Hinton has offered a
version aimed squarely at machines.

233
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:35,400
In an interview with the Globe and Mail,
he rejected the idea of an inner theater.

234
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:40,750
On his account, talk of subjective
experience is a way of describing what your

235
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:44,380
perceptual system is telling you
when it isn't working properly:

236
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:48,240
the state of the world that would have
to hold for your perception to be right.

237
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:52,290
Then he imagined a chatbot with a
camera, trained to point at objects.

238
00:15:52,340 --> 00:15:56,140
Put a prism in front of the lens,
and it points to the wrong place.

239
00:15:56,340 --> 00:16:00,860
Tell it about the prism, and it explains
that the object is really straight ahead,

240
00:16:01,100 --> 00:16:04,560
but it had the subjective experience
of it being off to one side.

241
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:09,850
Hinton's claim is that it uses the phrase
exactly as we do, and so has subjective

242
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:13,380
experience just as we
do. That seems too quick.

243
00:16:13,620 --> 00:16:15,780
The chatbot learned that phrase from us.

244
00:16:16,060 --> 00:16:20,160
It did, and that is what the machine
episodes will call the gaming problem:

245
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:24,560
a system trained on our words can
give our reports without our reasons.

246
00:16:25,220 --> 00:16:30,840
Blaise Agüera y Arcas, whom we met in episode
four, takes the same line as Graziano.

247
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:36,650
Attention is a real computation, he argues,
so a model of it is no more an illusion

248
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:42,080
than a chair is. Like Graziano, he accepts
the mechanism and resists the name.

249
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:49,070
Fiction got here early. Peter Watts's 2006
novel Blindsight, which drew openly on

250
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:53,900
Metzinger, sends a crew of modified humans
to meet aliens that are vastly capable

251
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:59,450
and, it seems, entirely non-conscious,
and asks whether consciousness might be a

252
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:02,360
costly side effect rather than
the crown of intelligence.

253
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:07,390
It's the doing-versus-feeling split as a
thriller, and Watts has made it free to

254
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:09,900
read online under a
Creative Commons license.

255
00:17:10,340 --> 00:17:12,900
Notice that the novel
works under either reading.

256
00:17:13,100 --> 00:17:16,470
A realist reads the aliens
as lacking experience.

257
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:21,270
An illusionist reads them as lacking the
self-model, and therefore lacking the

258
00:17:21,320 --> 00:17:25,160
illusion. Episode ten has
the real-world counterpart:

259
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:29,500
patients with blindsight, who respond
to what they say they cannot see.

260
00:17:30,580 --> 00:17:32,940
Now the objections, at full strength.

261
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:38,170
In 2018, on the New York Review of Books
website, the philosopher Galen Strawson

262
00:17:38,220 --> 00:17:42,550
called the denial of consciousness the
silliest claim ever made, aiming chiefly at

263
00:17:42,600 --> 00:17:46,260
Dennett. Dennett replied that
Strawson had misdescribed his view.

264
00:17:46,520 --> 00:17:48,660
Consciousness is real, he said.

265
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:51,200
It just isn't what
Strawson takes it to be.

266
00:17:51,800 --> 00:17:55,990
Strawson's answer was that what illusionists
deny, the qualitative character of

267
00:17:56,040 --> 00:17:59,100
experience, is exactly the
thing we know for certain.

268
00:17:59,440 --> 00:18:02,460
That's the core objection,
and it can be put in one line.

269
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:08,070
An illusion is a way things seem, and a
seeming is an experience, so illusionism

270
00:18:08,120 --> 00:18:10,320
presupposes the very thing it denies.

271
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:15,330
John Searle made the point that where
consciousness is concerned, the appearance is

272
00:18:15,380 --> 00:18:19,320
the reality. You can be wrong about
whether there's a tomato in front of you.

273
00:18:19,520 --> 00:18:22,360
You can't be wrong that it
seems to you that there is.

274
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:27,090
Frankish's reply is that a seeming can
be a representational state, a kind of

275
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:31,370
judgment-like state produced by
introspective mechanisms, without any extra

276
00:18:31,420 --> 00:18:35,860
phenomenal property. Representations can
misrepresent without themselves glowing.

277
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:39,780
And when you ask who is fooled,
the answer is the system itself:

278
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:45,030
its reasoning, memory, and report
mechanisms, with no little observer inside.

279
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:50,080
Strawson's rejoinder is that seeming to
have such qualities already is having them.

280
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:52,360
Let me put that in my language.

281
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:58,310
Illusionism says the brain's self-model is
lossy and confidently wrong about its own

282
00:18:58,360 --> 00:19:00,620
internals. That's not exotic.

283
00:19:00,960 --> 00:19:06,270
Every learned model of a complex system
is lossy, and nothing forces a system's

284
00:19:06,320 --> 00:19:08,020
model of itself to be accurate.

285
00:19:08,340 --> 00:19:12,900
That's a good translation, and it's why
many engineers find the view natural.

286
00:19:13,140 --> 00:19:16,320
But the philosopher Philip
Goff presses a different point.

287
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:21,160
Consciousness is the one thing each of us
knows more directly than anything else.

288
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:26,270
All the evidence for any scientific theory,
including the theory that experience is

289
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:28,920
an illusion, reaches
us through experience.

290
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:34,540
So illusionism carries a heavier burden of
proof than any ordinary empirical theory.

291
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:38,520
It asks us to doubt the medium
through which we know everything else.

292
00:19:38,660 --> 00:19:42,580
And there's the illusion problem
itself, which is not yet solved.

293
00:19:42,860 --> 00:19:47,270
Why would evolution build a self-model
that represents our states as ineffable and

294
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:51,240
intrinsic? Graziano says
simplification serves control.

295
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:54,360
Humphrey says the show makes
life feel worth living.

296
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:57,700
Dennett says the interface hides
what you don't need to see.

297
00:19:57,880 --> 00:20:01,060
These are promising stories,
not finished explanations.

298
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:05,460
A view that asks this much is
easy to dismiss from outside.

299
00:20:06,060 --> 00:20:11,350
The philosopher Joe Carlsmith's essay
Grokking Illusionism is a sustained attempt to

300
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:16,030
understand it from the inside instead,
and that is the right spirit in which to

301
00:20:16,080 --> 00:20:20,440
judge it. Why does this view
matter so much for this series?

302
00:20:20,660 --> 00:20:24,320
Because it changes the machine
question more than any other position.

303
00:20:24,560 --> 00:20:29,810
If illusionism is right, then asking whether
a machine is conscious becomes asking

304
00:20:29,860 --> 00:20:33,070
whether it has the relevant
kind of self-model:

305
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:37,420
introspective representations of its own
states as having phenomenal qualities,

306
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:41,440
produced for the right reasons, and
driving what it says about itself.

307
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:46,500
That's a question about mechanisms, and
interpretability research can, in principle,

308
00:20:46,940 --> 00:20:51,050
look for internal states that track the
system's own processing and feed its

309
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:55,760
self-reports. Episode twelve covers
early experiments of that kind.

310
00:20:56,040 --> 00:21:01,250
But notice the trap. A language model
trained on human writing can produce flawless

311
00:21:01,300 --> 00:21:06,310
reports of ineffable inner experience
without any such self-model, just by learning

312
00:21:06,360 --> 00:21:10,720
how we talk. So illusionism doesn't
make the machine question easy.

313
00:21:10,940 --> 00:21:14,980
It makes it empirical. The test
is not what the system says.

314
00:21:15,120 --> 00:21:20,910
It's why it says it. In 2023, Dennett's
own warning about AI was not about machines

315
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:25,710
that feel. It was about machines that pass
for people, which he called counterfeit

316
00:21:25,760 --> 00:21:30,710
people. We'll come back to that
warning in the final episode.

317
00:21:30,760 --> 00:21:32,770
Score it for me. Where does it stand?

318
00:21:32,820 --> 00:21:37,895
In its favor: parsimony, since it
adds no new ingredient to the world.

319
00:21:37,945 --> 00:21:42,231
Tractability, since it turns the deepest
question into the meta-problem, which

320
00:21:42,281 --> 00:21:47,749
science can work on. And the track record
of introspection, bad enough that trusting

321
00:21:47,799 --> 00:21:51,691
it on the biggest question
of all needs an argument.

322
00:21:51,741 --> 00:21:55,633
Against it: the seeming objection,
which illusionists have answered without

323
00:21:55,683 --> 00:22:00,320
silencing. The burden of proof, since it
asks us to doubt what seems most certain.

324
00:22:00,540 --> 00:22:04,620
And the illusion problem, which
remains a program rather than a result.

325
00:22:04,900 --> 00:22:09,950
What illusionism establishes, I think,
is that the hard problem's most powerful

326
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,740
intuitions could be the
outputs of a mechanism.

327
00:22:13,020 --> 00:22:15,120
It doesn't establish that they are.

328
00:22:15,440 --> 00:22:19,870
That's a real achievement, and it's why
the meta-problem comes back in the final

329
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:23,000
episode, as one of the things
that could change minds.

330
00:22:23,420 --> 00:22:25,590
Next episode, we lay out the whole map:

331
00:22:25,640 --> 00:22:30,790
every serious position on the hard problem,
what each one costs, and what each one

332
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:32,700
says about octopuses and machines.

333
00:22:33,160 --> 00:22:36,700
And at the end, a short, clearly
marked trip to the edges.
